CORE
main.c
--unwind 1 --rounds 2 --por
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
`goto_symex_statet::rename<L2>`'s per-element guard-conditioned rename for a
load at a symbolic index on a field-sensitivity-expanded array (added to fix
the 09-regions/06-symbeq false-alarm family: a thread touching `data[1]`
under `mutex[1]` must not be paired against a write under `mutex[0]`) adds
`index == k` as a guard conjunct before renaming each element `k`, so the
event lazy_po records for the elements the load does not touch carries that
condition. When the index renames to an actual constant -- a thread's own
parameter, here, always called with the same literal argument, so there is
no real ambiguity left -- that conjunct is a decided equality
(`renamed_index == k`) that the atomic-section read bookkeeping in
`l2_thread_read_encoding` does not fold away structurally. The generation it
mints for the never-touched element is then tied to its old value only
through an implication the solver discharges vacuously by setting that
decided-false guard false -- exactly what happens on the one real path --
leaving the element's value free. A later plain, constant-index read of that
element (here, an overflow-check instrumented invariant) sees the free
generation and a provably-true invariant is reported violated.

Reproducer: two threads call `bump(1)` (always index 1, never 0) inside an
atomic section; `checkInv`'s invariant only involves `arr[0]` (never written)
and `arr[1]`. Reported FAILED before this fix (`arr[0]` found unconstrained),
SUCCESSFUL after.

Real-corpus trigger: `elimination_backoff_stack` under `no-overflow`
(`checkInvariant.overflow.1`/`.2` at line 1543, `PushDone[0] + PushOpen[0]`)
-- `localPush1` is a function parameter always called with the literal
argument `1`, syntactically non-constant but always resolving to one after
renaming; same mechanism exactly. Verified via the real `deagle_escalate`
wrapper: `VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL` after this fix, matching the expected
answer.

See `atomic_section_const_index_framing_race`, the control: same
constant-resolved-index-under-atomic-section shape, but a third,
unprotected write actually does touch the "never-touched" element --
must stay FAILED before and after, proving the fix does not swallow a
real race in this same code path.
